.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]cbor::item\f[R] is a decoded tree, and \f[CR]cbor::encoder\f[R]
writes CBOR one data item per call.
\f[CR]decode\f[R] copies every string into one memory zone.
.SS cbor::item
One node of a tree from cbor::decode() or \f[CR]lazy::clone\f[R].
\f[CR]type\f[R] gives the kind of the node.
\f[CR]additional_information\f[R] gives the low 5 bits of the initial
byte (RFC 8949 3).
cbor::item::get() gives the value.
The constructor is private: only a decode makes an item.
An item owns no memory and has no destructor to run.
The tree owns every node and every string.
None.
\f[CR]type\f[R] and \f[CR]additional_information\f[R] are data members.
.SS cbor::item::get
Gives the value of the node when \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]K\f[R].
| K | Result | ||| | \f[CR]unsigned_integer\f[R],
\f[CR]negative_integer\f[R] | \f[CR]std::uint64_t\f[R], the argument | |
\f[CR]half_float\f[R], \f[CR]single_float\f[R], \f[CR]double_float\f[R]
| \f[CR]double\f[R] | | \f[CR]text_string\f[R] |
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] | | \f[CR]byte_string\f[R] |
\f[CR]std::span<std::byte const>\f[R] | | \f[CR]array\f[R] |
\f[CR]std::span<item const>\f[R], the elements | | \f[CR]map\f[R] |
\f[CR]std::span<item const>\f[R], each key and its value one after the
other | | \f[CR]tag\f[R], \f[CR]unsigned_bignum\f[R],
\f[CR]negative_bignum\f[R], \f[CR]typed_array\f[R] |
\f[CR]std::pair<std::uint64_t, item const *>\f[R]: the tag number and
the content | | \f[CR]false_value\f[R], \f[CR]true_value\f[R],
\f[CR]null\f[R], \f[CR]undefined\f[R], \f[CR]simple_value\f[R] |
\f[CR]cbor::simple_value\f[R] | A negative integer gives its argument n,
for the value \-1 \- n.
A view and a pointer in the result point into the tree.
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: \f[CR]type\f[R] is not \f[CR]K\f[R].
.SS cbor::decode
Decodes the one top\-level item in \f[CR]encoded\f[R] into a tree of
cbor::item.
It checks every head.
The tree holds a copy of each text and byte string, so it stays valid
after \f[CR]encoded\f[R] ends.
A bignum whose magnitude fits 64 bits becomes an integer node.
A tag 24 is a tag node, and cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item() gives its
decoded content.
Tags 28 and 29 stay tag nodes.
One leading tag 55799 is read as its content, so the root is that
content.
A second tag 55799 and a tag 55799 inside an item stay tag nodes.
.SS cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item
Gives the root of the item that a tag 24 (RFC 8949 3.4.5.1) holds in its
byte string.
\f[CR]tagged\f[R] is the tag node in \f[CR]tree\f[R].
cbor::decode() decodes that content when it decodes the tree, so the
call only looks it up.
.SS cbor::encode
Writes the tree at \f[CR]root\f[R] and gives the count of bytes written.
Each integer, length and tag number keeps the width of its head, from
\f[CR]additional_information\f[R].
A float keeps its width.
A tag 28 and a tag 29 are written as they are.
The target is one of these:
.IP \(bu 2
a container of bytes, for example \f[CR]std::string\f[R]: the call
appends; \- a \f[CR]std::span\f[R] of bytes: the call writes into it and
allocates nothing; \- an object with \f[CR]allocate(std::size_t)\f[R],
which gives a writer with \f[CR]append(std::string_view)\f[R] and
\f[CR]done(std::size_t)\f[R].
.SS cbor::encode_iov
Writes the tree at \f[CR]root\f[R] into a list of
\f[CR]struct iovec\f[R] pieces, not into one buffer.
The result gives the pieces in order and holds their storage and the
owners of the pieces that refer to strings.
The pieces are for sending only: \f[CR]writev\f[R], \f[CR]sendmsg\f[R],
\f[CR]IORING_OP_WRITEV\f[R] and \f[CR]IORING_OP_SENDMSG\f[R].
The program holds the \f[CR]ref\f[R] until the call returns or until the
last completion of the request.
The bytes are those of cbor::encode().
The function exists where \f[CR]<sys/uio.h>\f[R] exists.
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: the tree is deeper than the limit.
A piece that refers to the string of an owner points into memory that
the owner holds as const; a call that writes into the pieces, such as
\f[CR]readv\f[R], is a wrong use.
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>
Writes CBOR one data item per call, in the order of the bytes.
The encoder counts nothing: the counts of \f[CR]array\f[R] and
\f[CR]map\f[R] come from the program.
The target is one of these:
.IP \(bu 2
a container of bytes, for example \f[CR]std::string\f[R]: the encoder
appends; \- a \f[CR]std::span\f[R] of bytes: the encoder writes into it
and allocates nothing; \- an cbor::iov_writer: the encoder gives pieces;
\- an object with \f[CR]allocate(std::size_t)\f[R], which gives a writer
with \f[CR]append(std::string_view)\f[R] and
\f[CR]done(std::size_t)\f[R].
The encoder does not write tags 28 and 29.
Each call gives \f[CR]std::expected<void, error>\f[R].
After an error, and after cbor::encoder::finish(), each call throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
A copy does not compile.
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::unsigned_integer
Writes \f[CR]value\f[R] as an unsigned integer (major type 0).
The head takes the width of \f[CR]T\f[R]: one byte of argument for
\f[CR]std::uint8_t\f[R], eight for \f[CR]std::uint64_t\f[R], also when a
shorter head holds the value.
\f[CR]T\f[R] is an unsigned integer type.
\f[CR]bool\f[R] and the character types do not compile.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::negative_integer
Writes a negative integer (major type 1) with the argument
\f[CR]argument\f[R].
The value is \-1 \- \f[CR]argument\f[R].
The head takes the width of \f[CR]T\f[R].
\f[CR]T\f[R] is an unsigned integer type.
\f[CR]bool\f[R] and the character types do not compile.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::byte_string
Writes \f[CR]bytes\f[R] as a byte string, with the shortest head for the
length.
Over an cbor::iov_writer, the last three forms do not copy 32 bytes or
more: the result keeps the moved vector or the owner.
Over any other target, each form copies the bytes.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A null \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::text_string
Writes \f[CR]text\f[R] as a text string, with the shortest head for the
length.
The call does not check UTF\-8.
Over an cbor::iov_writer, the last three forms do not copy a text of 32
bytes or more: the result keeps the moved string or the owner.
Over any other target, each form copies the text.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A null \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::array
Writes the head of an array (major type 4) with \f[CR]count\f[R]
elements, with the shortest head for \f[CR]count\f[R].
The next calls write the elements.
The encoder does not count them: a wrong count gives bytes that are not
one item.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::map
Writes the head of a map (major type 5) with \f[CR]count\f[R] pairs,
with the shortest head for \f[CR]count\f[R].
The next calls write each key and then its value.
The encoder does not count them: a wrong count gives bytes that are not
one item.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::tag
Writes the head of a tag (major type 6) with the tag number
\f[CR]number\f[R], with the shortest head.
The next call writes the content.
The call does not check that the content fits the tag.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::simple_value
Writes a simple value (major type 7), for example
\f[CR]false_value\f[R], \f[CR]true_value\f[R], \f[CR]null\f[R] or
\f[CR]undefined\f[R].
A value from 0 to 23 takes one byte, and a value from 32 to 255 takes
two.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::floating_point
Writes \f[CR]value\f[R] as a float in the width of \f[CR]T\f[R]: 16, 32
or 64 bits.
It does not choose a shorter form.
A NaN keeps its bits.
\f[CR]T\f[R] is an IEEE 754 type of 16, 32 or 64 bits.
Another float type does not compile.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
A call after an error or after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::typed_array
Writes a typed array (RFC 8746): the tag \f[CR]value.tag\f[R] and then
\f[CR]value.bytes\f[R] as a byte string.
The bytes are copied as they are, in the byte order that the tag names.
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R] over a span that is too short.
The error of a writer.
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::finish
Ends the encoder.
It writes the buffer of the encoder to the target and calls
\f[CR]done\f[R] of a writer.
It gives the count of bytes written, or the pieces over an
cbor::iov_writer.
After the call, each call of the encoder throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R], \f[CR]value_too_large\f[R],
\f[CR]not_enough_memory\f[R], \f[CR]io_error\f[R]: the error of the
target.
A second \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::iov_writer
A target of cbor::encoder that collects pieces, not one buffer.
The encoder writes heads and short strings into blocks of 16 KiB.
A text or a byte string of 32 bytes or more that the program hands over
with its owner becomes a piece of its own, with no copy.
cbor::encoder::finish() gives the pieces as \f[CR]struct iovec\f[R], in
a \f[CR]ref\f[R] that holds the blocks and the owners.
The pieces are for sending only, as cbor::encode_iov() says.
The class exists where \f[CR]<sys/uio.h>\f[R] exists.
The class has no public member function.
A write after \f[CR]finish\f[R] throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SH ERRORS
.SS cbor::decode
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the item is not complete, or a count is
larger than the bytes that are left
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: a head is not well\-formed.
A byte after the item is not read, so the input can be a CBOR sequence
(RFC 8742)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]indefinite_length\f[R]: a string, an array or a map has an
indefinite length
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: the item is deeper than the limit
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]reserved_simple_value\f[R],
\f[CR]inadmissible_type_for_tag_content\f[R]: a value that RFC 8949 or
RFC 8746 does not allow
.SS cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: the content of the tag 24 is not a byte
string
.SS cbor::encode
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R]: the span is too short
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: the tree is deeper than the limit
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]value_too_large\f[R], \f[CR]not_enough_memory\f[R],
\f[CR]io_error\f[R]: the error of a writer, from its
\f[CR]std::errc\f[R]
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::simple_value
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]reserved_simple_value\f[R]: \f[CR]value\f[R] is from 24 to 31 (RFC
8949 3.3)
.SS cbor::encoder<Target>::typed_array
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]unsupported_value\f[R]: \f[CR]value.tag\f[R] is not the tag of a
typed array
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]inadmissible_type_for_tag_content\f[R]: the count of bytes is not
a multiple of the size of one element
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS cbor::item
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string with [1, {\(dqa\(dq: 2}]\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const kind = (*tree)\->type;                              \f[I]// cbor::type::array\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const count = (*tree)\->get<cbor::type::array>()\->size();  \f[I]// 2\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::item::get
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string with {\(dqa\(dq: \-3}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const pairs = *(*tree)\->get<cbor::type::map>();
\f[B]auto\f[R] const key = pairs[0].get<cbor::type::text_string>();        \f[I]// \(dqa\(dq\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const argument = pairs[1].get<cbor::type::negative_integer>(); \f[I]// 2, for \-3\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const other = pairs[1].get<cbor::type::unsigned_integer>();    \f[I]// incorrect_type\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::decode
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string with [\(dqhi\(dq, 1]\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const count = (*tree)\->get<cbor::type::array>()\->size();  \f[I]// 2\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const first = cbor::decode(\(dq\(rsx01\(rsx02\(dqs);                    \f[I]// the item 1; the byte 02 is not read\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string with 24(h\(aq6161\(aq), a tag 24 over the text \(dqa\(dq\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const inner = cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item(*tree, **tree);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const text = inner\->get().get<cbor::type::text_string>();  \f[I]// \(dqa\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::encode
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string with [1, \(dqa\(dq]\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
std::string out;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const size = cbor::encode(**tree, out);  \f[I]// 4; out == bytes\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::encode_iov
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string with [1, \(dqa\(dq]\f[R]
